# Pinned `spec` revision the conformance runner validates against.
#
# The runner refuses to run if the spec checkout it is given is not at this
# commit (override with --allow-spec-drift when deliberately testing against
# unreleased vocabulary). Without a pin the suite silently tracks whatever is
# on spec `main`, so a run that passed yesterday can pass today for a
# different reason.
#
# To re-pin: check out the new spec revision, put its full SHA and its
# VOCAB_VERSIONS line below, run `make -C . conformance` (or
# `scripts/run_conformance.py`), and record the resulting counts in the PR.

# Pinned at the MERGE commit of jayostis/spec PR #42 on `main`
# (core v3.12 / health v2.8 / clinical v1.19 / coverage v1.8 / checkup v3.4 /
# pots v1.4).
#
# `repo=` IS STILL THE FORK, and still deliberately. This commit is not on
# the-cascade-protocol/spec and is not an ancestor of its `main`, which sits at
# 97a5388. core v3.9 through v3.12, clinical v1.18 and v1.19, coverage v1.7 and
# v1.8, and checkup v3.4 exist today only on the fork. CI clones `repo=` and
# checks out `commit=`, so naming the org here would fail before a single
# fixture ran. Move `repo=` back to the org in the same commit that re-pins to
# an org SHA, once this vocabulary lands there.
#
# WHAT KEEPS THIS COMMIT REACHABLE, because a branch tip is not a guarantee:
# THREE tags point at this exact SHA -- `vocab/core-v3.12`,
# `vocab/coverage-v1.8` and `vocab/checkup-v3.4` -- verified with `git ls-remote`
# before pinning. A tag holds the object even if the fork's `main` is later reset
# or rebased onto upstream, which is the normal end of a fork workflow and would
# otherwise leave every CI run -- including runs of unrelated PRs -- dying at
# `git checkout` on an object that has been garbage-collected. Do not re-pin to
# a fork SHA that no tag points at.
#
# THIS PIN MOVES FOUR VOCABULARIES: core 3.10 -> 3.12, coverage 1.7 -> 1.8,
# checkup 3.3 -> 3.4. health, clinical and pots are unchanged.
#
# ONLY ONE SHAPE FILE CHANGED, and only one fixture was affected by it.
# `core.shapes.ttl` is the sole `*.shapes.ttl` touched between the two pins; the
# other three version steps are ONTOLOGY-ONLY. That asymmetry is the whole story
# of this pin and is why the constraint-check total does not move.
#
#   core v3.11  OPENED the cascade:consentScope value set. D-CONSENT-1 ratified
#               it as open -- "sh:in at sh:Warning at most, never sh:Violation
#               ... a closed list missing a member rejects conformant data" --
#               and v3.10 had published the opposite, one member at
#               sh:Violation. The shape SPLIT rather than softening: sh:nodeKind
#               / sh:minCount / sh:maxCount keep sh:Violation in a block with no
#               sh:in, and sh:in moved alone to a second block at sh:Warning.
#   core v3.12  cascade:ConflictDetail and cascade:AIDiscardedExtraction gain
#               rdfs:subClassOf prov:Entity. No shape change.
#   coverage    v1.8: coverage:ClaimRecord and coverage:BenefitStatement gain
#               rdfs:subClassOf prov:Entity. coverage.shapes.ttl untouched.
#   checkup     v3.4: a settings class declared as a record class. No shape
#               change.
#
# The three ontology-only steps are one change -- jayostis/spec#13, seven record
# classes declaring the PROV superclass the class-coverage gate reads.
#
# WHAT MOVING THE PIN DID: exactly one fixture was affected across all 177, and
# this PR converts it rather than leaving it red.
# `clinical/social-history-consent-scope-wrong-value` asserted a sh:Violation
# that core v3.11 deliberately stopped performing, so it went pass ->
# NO_VIOLATION. It is renamed `.INVALID.ttl` -> `.WARN.ttl`, which is the claim
# the vocabulary now supports: the value set still FIRES, at Warning, and
# nothing rejects the record. Its data is unchanged.
#
# MEASURED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, because a rename that is merely cosmetic would
# look identical from one side:
#
#   old pin 40e581f, fixture as .INVALID.ttl : 151 passed / 26 failed / 177 total
#   new pin 3362861, fixture as .WARN.ttl    : 151 passed / 26 failed / 177 total
#   new pin 3362861, fixture as .INVALID.ttl : 150 passed / 27 failed  (NO_VIOLATION)
#   old pin 40e581f, fixture as .WARN.ttl    : 150 passed / 27 failed  (VIOLATIONS)
#
# The last two lines are the point. The fixture is correct for exactly one
# vocabulary version in each polarity, so the rename is tied to core v3.11 and
# is not a cosmetic choice. No other fixture changed verdict, gained a warning
# or lost one.
#
# 63,817 constraint checks at BOTH pins, unchanged, and that is expected rather
# than suspicious: the consent-scope split turned one four-constraint block into
# a three-plus-one pair, which evaluates the same four constraints. The shape
# corpus did move -- 7,753 -> 7,762 shapes triples (the split) and 149 -> 156
# subclass axioms (spec#13's seven declarations) -- while node shapes stay at
# 126, since no shape was added or removed.
#
# WHAT REACHES NO FIXTURE, named here rather than left implied. core v3.12,
# coverage v1.8 and checkup v3.4 are ontology-only and add ZERO constraint
# checks to this suite. The seven classes they declare as record data are the
# same seven that no shape targets, so the declarations are invisible here until
# `spec` shapes them. checkup v3.4's settings class is inside the intake-form
# nesting question that #5 is blocked on. See VOCAB_VERSIONS for the per-row
# detail.
#
# The 26 failures are the same 26 (fixture, reason) pairs KNOWN_FAILURES.json
# already carried, so no entry is added and none is removed. Only the file's
# `specPin` scalar advances, which check_baseline.py requires and refuses to
# run without.

repo=https://github.com/jayostis/spec
commit=3362861f03fc153bb809f9c51ccbccb9bb6718a5
vocab=core=3.12 health=2.8 clinical=1.19 coverage=1.8 checkup=3.4 pots=1.4
